const controlCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The feedback loop', note: 'Measure the output, compare to the setpoint, correct the input. The thermostat, the cruise control, the pancreas (the biology lesson\'s homeostasis) — feedback is how systems hold a target in a changing world.' },
    { math: '2 · Proportional–integral–derivative', note: 'P: correct by current error. I: correct by accumulated error (kills steady-state offset). D: correct by error trend (damps oscillation). PID — three gains tuned by method (Ziegler–Nichols) — runs ~95% of industrial loops.' },
    { math: '3 · Stability: the loop can fight itself', note: 'Too much gain or lag → oscillation → instability. The mathematics (poles, Bode plots) predicts it before the hardware shakes: control theory is the discipline of loops that behave.' },
    { math: '4 · Beyond single loops', note: 'State-space (model the whole system), MPC (optimise over a horizon — chemical plants and now some cars), adaptive and robust control (handle what you cannot model). Modern control is a quiet pillar of automation, robotics and aerospace (the AI lesson\'s embodied systems).' },
  ],
};

const controlQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'A 10-bit ADC measuring 0–5 V resolves steps of…', choices: ['5 mV', '4.9 mV', '0.5 V', '49 mV'], answer: 1, why: '5 V ÷ 2¹⁰ = 5/1024 ≈ 4.9 mV. ADC resolution is the smallest signal detail the loop can even see — and it is chosen against the sensor\'s real precision, not blindly.' },
    { q: 'Why is an anti-aliasing filter placed before the ADC?', choices: ['to amplify weak signals', 'to remove frequencies above half the sampling rate, which would otherwise masquerade as slow ones', 'to save power', 'to convert digital to analog'], answer: 1, why: 'Sampling folds frequencies above Nyquist back down as impostors (aliasing) that no software can undo afterwards. The analogue filter must be analogue — it acts before the damage is done.' },
    { q: 'Pure proportional control leaves a steady-state offset because…', choices: ['the sensor is slow', 'the corrective action is proportional to error, so zero error would mean zero action — the system settles where error still drives just enough output', 'P gain is always too low', 'offset is random'], answer: 1, why: 'If the plant needs sustained output to hold setpoint, P-only control needs a standing error to produce it. The integral term fixes exactly this: it accumulates error until the offset vanishes.' },
    { q: 'In ladder logic, a motor "seal-in" contact exists so that…', choices: ['the motor spins faster', 'the motor keeps running after the momentary start button is released', 'the fuse never blows', 'the sensor stays calibrated'], answer: 1, why: 'A relay contact wired in parallel with the start button latches the circuit once started; the stop button breaks it. Logic implemented in contacts — the PLC\'s ancestor still runs most factories.' },
  ],
};

